<.shell title="Stat switch" current={:stat_switch}>
  <h1>Stat switch</h1>
  <p class="lead">A list's split as counts, each one a filter.</p>

  <div class="demo">
    <.stat_switch label="Awards">
      <:view patch="#" count={171}>All</:view>
      <:view patch="#" current={true} count={131}>Active</:view>
      <:view patch="#" count={40}>Expired</:view>
    </.stat_switch>
    <.filter_bar
      for={sample_filters()}
      id="stat-switch-demo"
      count="131 members"
      repeats={true}
      onsubmit="return false"
    >
      <:search field={sample_filters()[:q]} />
    </.filter_bar>
  </div>

  <h3>With markers</h3>
  <div class="demo">
    <.stat_switch label="Kind">
      <:view patch="#" current={true} count={212}>All</:view>
      <:view patch="#" count={70} marker="incident">Incidents</:view>
      <:view patch="#" count={98} marker="exercise">Exercises</:view>
      <:view patch="#" count={44} marker="event">Events</:view>
    </.stat_switch>
  </div>

  <h2>When to use</h2>
  <p>
    A list that splits one obvious way into 2 to 5 parts, where how many are in each part is
    worth knowing: a qualification's active and expired awards, or who signed up for an
    activity. Each part shows the same columns.
  </p>

  <h2>When not to use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>More than 5 parts. Use a menu in a <a href={page_path(:filter_bar)}>filter bar</a>.</li>
    <li>
      Parts that need their own columns or their own button, such as the door's arrivals and
      no-shows. Give each its own section.
    </li>
    <li>Pages about one record. Use <a href={page_path(:tabs)}>tabs</a>.</li>
    <li>Numbers that don't filter anything. Use a <a href={page_path(:stat)}>stat</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Each count is a link that patches the page, so the URL says which part is showing.
      Each is a grey tile, all of it the link. The current one is light blue with a primary
      bar at its foot. There is no rule under the counts: the tiles show their own edges.
    </li>
    <li>All comes first. A page can open on another part, as awards open on Active.</li>
    <li>
      A count is the rows in its part before the search, and after any other filter. A part
      with none shows a grey 0 and stays selectable.
    </li>
    <li>
      When the rows show their part with a marker, the count's label has the same one. The
      list's headings carry no counts.
    </li>
    <li>
      With one part showing, drop a column that would say the same thing on every row. In
      All, grey a part kept for history, such as expired awards.
    </li>
    <li>
      A filter bar goes under it, and searches within the part showing. Its count shows only
      while the search or a filter leaves fewer rows than the current tile: set
      <code>repeats</code>
      when the two match.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;.stat_switch label="…"&gt;</code>
    with a <code>:view</code>
    slot per part, each with its <code>patch</code>, <code>current</code>, and <code>count</code>, and a
    <code>marker</code>
    for a coloured square, such as <code>marker="incident"</code>. The square is
    <code>.marker</code>
    and a <code>.marker-…</code>
    class that sets <code>--c</code>, in <code>badge.css</code>
    beside the <a href={page_path(:tag)}>tags</a>.
  </p>
</.shell>
